## What you are building

A constrained decoding strategy (CSD) is a decoding loop, written as a Dafny
method body, that produces output token by token by repeatedly calling the
helper API above. At each step the strategy is either **outside** a
constrained span (free generation, any token allowed) or **inside** one
(parser-valid tokens only, tracked via `insideConstrained` /
`currentConstrained`). A visible delimiter span is the part of the output
between an opening marker like `<<` and a closing marker like `>>`; entering
one switches the loop from free generation to parser-constrained generation,
and closing it switches back. Track just enough state — which mode you're in,
what has been generated inside the current span, and any counters your
control logic needs — to decide the next helper call correctly and to make
each helper's precondition provable at the point you call it.

## Output format

Return exactly the Dafny method body: rationale block, proof sketch block,
then body statements — nothing else surrounds it.
Begin your output with `// CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN` — no prose, no preamble, no Markdown fence above it.

